direction: right

title: |md
  ## Storing a kernel pointer across program invocations: kptr
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

problem: "Problem: tp_btf hands you a *task_struct" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  p_body: |md
    The pointer is **trusted only for the
    duration of this BPF program execution.**
    After the program returns, the task
    might be freed.

    Saving the pointer in a map directly
    is rejected by the verifier:
    'cannot reference object of type X
     in this map'.
  |
}

solution: "Solution: refcounted kptr in a map" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  s_body: |md
    ```c
    struct val { struct task_struct __kptr *t; };

    struct {
      __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_HASH);
      __type(value, struct val);
    } m SEC(".maps");
    ```

    - `__kptr` annotation tells verifier
      this slot holds a refcounted pointer
    - Use `bpf_kptr_xchg(slot, new)` to
      atomically swap; old must be released
  |
}

flow: "Lifecycle" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  f_body: |md
    1. acquire kfunc returns refcount=1, ref id N
    2. xchg into map: ref id N closed,
       map slot now owns the refcount
    3. lookup later: kptr_xchg(slot, NULL)
       returns the kptr; ref id M opens
    4. release with bpf_task_release: ref id M closed

    All transitions verifier-checked.
    No leaks, no UAFs.
  |
}

problem -> solution -> flow
